1
00:00:01,187 --> 00:00:03,727
(The Talk of the Town)

2
00:00:04,057 --> 00:00:05,587
Nice to see you, everyone.

3
00:00:06,357 --> 00:00:07,796
It's been a while, Lee Jung.

4
00:00:07,797 --> 00:00:09,196
- Gosh, it's been a while. - Welcome.

5
00:00:09,197 --> 00:00:11,226
- It's so nice to see you. - Hello.

6
00:00:11,227 --> 00:00:12,767
How did your friends react after your episode aired?

7
00:00:13,267 --> 00:00:15,137
A lot of my friends reached out to me.

8
00:00:15,737 --> 00:00:18,207
My friends and acquaintances commented that...

9
00:00:18,607 --> 00:00:21,037
I had a successful career now.

10
00:00:21,907 --> 00:00:23,006
(Unlike her charismatic presence on the job,)

11
00:00:23,007 --> 00:00:24,947
(she spent a wholesome day with Actor Roh Yoon Seo.)

12
00:00:25,177 --> 00:00:26,977
(Her episode went viral thanks to her unexpected charm.)

13
00:00:27,147 --> 00:00:28,546
(Lee Jung looks like a sweet girl. She's so kind and adorable.)

14
00:00:28,547 --> 00:00:30,086
(Can you star on the show again? An hour was too short.)

15
00:00:30,087 --> 00:00:31,716
- Dudu went viral too. - Right.

16
00:00:31,717 --> 00:00:32,916
- He's such a cute dog. - Right.

17
00:00:32,917 --> 00:00:35,857
- I also... Yes, he's so healthy. - Is he doing well?

18
00:00:35,886 --> 00:00:37,226
He's still adorable.

19
00:00:37,427 --> 00:00:38,527
What about the air conditioner?

20
00:00:38,587 --> 00:00:39,626
- Oh, the air conditioner. - Yes.

21
00:00:39,627 --> 00:00:40,927
- Did you fix it? - That also went viral.

22
00:00:41,657 --> 00:00:42,897
(Startled)

23
00:00:43,097 --> 00:00:44,497
- What? There's a leak. - What's going on?

24
00:00:45,067 --> 00:00:48,697
(All of a sudden, water started to leak from the air conditioner.)

25
00:00:49,237 --> 00:00:52,166
My landlord fixed the air conditioner...

26
00:00:52,167 --> 00:00:54,206
- after watching my episode. - What?

27
00:00:54,207 --> 00:00:55,877
- The landlord? - I see. The landlord?

28
00:00:56,007 --> 00:00:58,007
- That's a relief. - Right.

29
00:00:58,447 --> 00:01:00,717
We actually have more good news to share.

30
00:01:00,947 --> 00:01:02,746
This year, our program won...

31
00:01:02,747 --> 00:01:04,186
the Grand Award at the Brand of the Year Awards...

32
00:01:04,187 --> 00:01:06,046
for four consecutive years...

33
00:01:06,047 --> 00:01:08,456
in the observation-based variety show category.

34
00:01:08,457 --> 00:01:10,186
- Thank you. - My gosh.

35
00:01:10,187 --> 00:01:12,257
- Congratulations. - Winning it once is a huge feat.

36
00:01:12,427 --> 00:01:14,657
- Congratulations. - Congratulations.

37
00:01:14,797 --> 00:01:15,996
It's more meaningful...

38
00:01:15,997 --> 00:01:17,696
because our program won it four years in a row.

39
00:01:17,697 --> 00:01:20,296
We'll work harder to bring you more entertainment.

40
00:01:20,297 --> 00:01:21,497
(We'll do our best to make a better show.)

41
00:01:22,537 --> 00:01:25,337
(The Cheat Day of Self-Esteem)

42
00:01:25,907 --> 00:01:27,776
(Someone is standing on her toes.)

43
00:01:27,777 --> 00:01:28,806
Gosh, that hurts.

44
00:01:28,807 --> 00:01:29,837
(It's Na Rae?)

45
00:01:31,077 --> 00:01:32,207
(Groaning)

46
00:01:32,977 --> 00:01:34,346
- Gosh, that hurts a lot. - Why are you wearing...

47
00:01:34,347 --> 00:01:35,446
- a military-issued shirt? - Why are you wearing that?

48
00:01:35,447 --> 00:01:37,246
- Where did you get that shirt? - You can buy it in stores.

49
00:01:37,247 --> 00:01:38,446
- I just bought it online. - There are military stores.

50
00:01:38,447 --> 00:01:39,646
- Really? - Yes.

51
00:01:39,647 --> 00:01:41,416
I thought you didn't return yours when you got discharged.

52
00:01:41,417 --> 00:01:43,327
- Gosh, I must return everything. - You did, right?

53
00:01:43,527 --> 00:01:46,286
That shirt is great for absorbing sweat.

54
00:01:46,287 --> 00:01:47,726
- That's right. - It's so breathable.

55
00:01:47,727 --> 00:01:49,796
- Is that so? - That's the best summer shirt.

56
00:01:49,797 --> 00:01:51,526
It's super functional. I get hot easily.

57
00:01:51,527 --> 00:01:52,966
- You know? Right. - And the snail cream.

58
00:01:52,967 --> 00:01:54,166
That's the perfect color on you.

59
00:01:54,167 --> 00:01:55,867
Right, that color is perfect.

60
00:01:56,937 --> 00:01:58,706
I didn't even get pimples during puberty.

61
00:01:58,707 --> 00:02:00,376
Why am I getting them when I'm 40?

62
00:02:00,377 --> 00:02:03,547
(Sergeant Park is fighting a battle against the pimples.)

63
00:02:04,607 --> 00:02:05,676
(Smothered)

64
00:02:05,677 --> 00:02:07,817
- I shouldn't have touched them. - Why not wear the camouflage cream?

65
00:02:09,117 --> 00:02:10,416
I put on some wash-off face mask.

66
00:02:10,417 --> 00:02:11,887
My skin has been acting up lately.

67
00:02:14,517 --> 00:02:16,057
(Grabbing)

68
00:02:17,187 --> 00:02:18,627
(Giggling)

69
00:02:22,997 --> 00:02:24,327
It came out nicely.

70
00:02:27,137 --> 00:02:28,597
(Staring)

71
00:02:29,867 --> 00:02:31,436
(Looking around)

72
00:02:31,437 --> 00:02:34,007
This looks amazing. I want to do this right.

73
00:02:34,777 --> 00:02:36,307
- I wish it was a bit bigger. - What's that?

74
00:02:36,377 --> 00:02:38,206
- My gosh. That photo. - It's your wedding photo.

75
00:02:38,207 --> 00:02:39,677
That would've been the perfect size.

76
00:02:40,177 --> 00:02:42,176
A lot of people made seaweed soup after watching this episode.

77
00:02:42,177 --> 00:02:44,587
People have been talking about this for quite some time.

78
00:02:46,587 --> 00:02:48,716
(She carefully displays it in her house.)

79
00:02:48,717 --> 00:02:49,757
This looks good.

80
00:02:51,227 --> 00:02:53,526
But it looks a bit small.

81
00:02:53,527 --> 00:02:54,856
That's a great picture.

82
00:02:54,857 --> 00:02:55,926
I got it in a small size.

83
00:02:55,927 --> 00:02:57,567
- You did that on purpose? - Yes.

84
00:02:58,097 --> 00:02:59,196
Yes, it looks beautiful.

85
00:02:59,197 --> 00:03:02,267
(Yes, it looks beautiful.)

86
00:03:03,037 --> 00:03:05,467
(Staring)

87
00:03:06,007 --> 00:03:07,337
It came out nicely.

88
00:03:07,537 --> 00:03:09,106
I really wanted to get that developed.

89
00:03:09,107 --> 00:03:10,376
- You had to. - It's a great picture.

90
00:03:10,377 --> 00:03:11,446
- Right, I had to. - Of course.

91
00:03:11,447 --> 00:03:12,546
It's like a photo from a magazine.

92
00:03:12,547 --> 00:03:13,816
Wear that to the year-end awards ceremony.

93
00:03:13,817 --> 00:03:15,276
- That's not a bad idea. - As the broom-to-be?

94
00:03:15,277 --> 00:03:16,446
- Isn't it a good idea? - That's a great concept.

95
00:03:16,447 --> 00:03:18,316
Upgrade the broom-to-be outfit and wear it to the ceremony.

96
00:03:18,317 --> 00:03:19,346
- Yes. - I can do that.

97
00:03:19,347 --> 00:03:20,617
Wear a men's hairstyle.

98
00:03:21,557 --> 00:03:23,187
- Get the buzz cut? - Get the buzz cut.

99
00:03:23,427 --> 00:03:24,556
- Get the buzz cut. - Get the buzz cut.

100
00:03:24,557 --> 00:03:25,886
- Like the two-block haircut. - As the broom-to-be.

101
00:03:25,887 --> 00:03:27,526
Remember the villain in "Mazinger Z?"

102
00:03:27,527 --> 00:03:29,156
- Baron Ashura? - Baron Ashura. Yes.

103
00:03:29,157 --> 00:03:30,426
- What's going on? - Yes.

104
00:03:30,427 --> 00:03:31,866
- Half of it. - Half man and half woman.

105
00:03:31,867 --> 00:03:33,026
(Her broom-to-be concept is improving.)

106
00:03:33,027 --> 00:03:34,037
Come on.

107
00:03:35,697 --> 00:03:37,307
This looks hip.

108
00:03:39,207 --> 00:03:40,306
(She goes downstairs after displaying her photo.)

109
00:03:40,307 --> 00:03:41,607
She does look like a commissioned officer.

110
00:03:42,437 --> 00:03:44,647
She's getting nostalgic at the military locker.

111
00:03:44,807 --> 00:03:45,907
The military locker?

112
00:03:49,647 --> 00:03:51,387
- What? - Is someone in your house?

113
00:03:52,247 --> 00:03:55,057
(She hurriedly grabs a few beverage cans.)

114
00:03:55,357 --> 00:03:56,927
(Glancing)

115
00:03:57,527 --> 00:03:58,786
(People are moving around busily in her house.)

116
00:03:58,787 --> 00:04:00,427
- Are you renovating your house? - Gosh.

117
00:04:02,527 --> 00:04:03,626
(They're even utilizing professional equipment.)

118
00:04:03,627 --> 00:04:04,827
That's to keep the line parallel.

119
00:04:05,397 --> 00:04:06,936
(Outdoor Ad Installation Agency)

120
00:04:06,937 --> 00:04:08,937
Why is there an agency for installing outdoor ads?

121
00:04:09,397 --> 00:04:11,136
What reason could they possibly have to be at her house?

122
00:04:11,137 --> 00:04:12,307
The outdoor ads...

123
00:04:12,937 --> 00:04:15,077
(The construction site seems unusual.)

124
00:04:15,477 --> 00:04:17,806
This is all I have to offer you in the house.

125
00:04:17,807 --> 00:04:20,046
- Have a cool drink while working. - Thank you.

126
00:04:20,047 --> 00:04:22,447
- Have a drink. - Gosh, hello.

127
00:04:23,177 --> 00:04:24,317
- Hello. - I'm a fan.

128
00:04:24,517 --> 00:04:25,817
Gosh, thank you.

129
00:04:26,587 --> 00:04:29,717
By the way, it is indeed huge.

130
00:04:31,327 --> 00:04:32,487
What's huge?

131
00:04:33,087 --> 00:04:34,157
It's like an artwork.

132
00:04:34,397 --> 00:04:35,497
- What's that? - What is that?

133
00:04:36,257 --> 00:04:38,026
This puts me in a solemn mood.

134
00:04:38,027 --> 00:04:39,096
What's that?

135
00:04:39,097 --> 00:04:40,897
(Overwhelming)

136
00:04:44,467 --> 00:04:45,537
Come on.

137
00:04:46,467 --> 00:04:49,707
Having that in your home is the same as Hyun Moo having his leaves.

138
00:04:49,937 --> 00:04:51,606
(Moomoo brought tree leaves into his home.)

139
00:04:51,607 --> 00:04:53,006
Come on. This is completely different.

140
00:04:53,007 --> 00:04:54,776
Don't insult my tree leaves.

141
00:04:54,777 --> 00:04:56,117
They can purify the air, at least.

142
00:04:56,317 --> 00:04:58,346
Seriously. They're both on the extreme ends.

143
00:04:58,347 --> 00:04:59,816
This is coming right from my heart.

144
00:04:59,817 --> 00:05:02,357
- Are you trying to battle me? - What do you mean? How absurd.

145
00:05:03,057 --> 00:05:04,356
Recently,

146
00:05:04,357 --> 00:05:06,727
I created an art piece with my concept as the broom-to-be.

147
00:05:07,327 --> 00:05:11,267
I wanted to celebrate my piece in a proper manner.

148
00:05:11,697 --> 00:05:13,996
This guy is almost an expert in this kind of field.

149
00:05:13,997 --> 00:05:16,037
Ro Hong Chul referred me to this agency.

150
00:05:17,107 --> 00:05:19,067
- He can be really excessive. - Right.

151
00:05:20,167 --> 00:05:24,677
There's a famous artist in the world of neon signs.

152
00:05:24,907 --> 00:05:27,846
He said this artist might be able to execute my vision.

153
00:05:27,847 --> 00:05:30,547
So I personally sent him a direct message.

154
00:05:30,687 --> 00:05:32,047
That's how I ended up with this art piece.

155
00:05:32,417 --> 00:05:34,186
Did you also install lightbulbs?

156
00:05:34,187 --> 00:05:35,317
- Yes. The neon signs. - The lights...

157
00:05:35,757 --> 00:05:36,757
- Oh, no. - Hong Chul said...

158
00:05:36,758 --> 00:05:38,857
he wanted to work with this artist.

159
00:05:38,987 --> 00:05:40,626
But he never got a chance to do that.

160
00:05:40,627 --> 00:05:42,356
He told me I should have a meeting with him.

161
00:05:42,357 --> 00:05:44,127
That's how he introduced me to Mr. Neon.

162
00:05:44,327 --> 00:05:45,526
- Mr. Neon. - Yes.

163
00:05:45,527 --> 00:05:46,866
Your house already attracts a lot of bugs.

164
00:05:46,867 --> 00:05:48,397
- You'll turn on those lights? - My gosh.

165
00:05:49,097 --> 00:05:50,607
They will attract moths.

166
00:05:50,967 --> 00:05:52,636
Her wedding picture...

167
00:05:52,637 --> 00:05:54,277
crossed over to modern art.

168
00:05:55,837 --> 00:05:57,346
I can use the neon signs to portray my chest hair.

169
00:05:57,347 --> 00:05:58,906
(Horrified)

170
00:05:58,907 --> 00:06:00,347
Your chest hair will curl up. No.

171
00:06:00,847 --> 00:06:03,247
- You'll burn your chest hair. - How many strands will you need?

172
00:06:03,317 --> 00:06:04,987
(Moomoo wants to dabble in modern art too.)

173
00:06:05,317 --> 00:06:07,457
This is great. I love it.

174
00:06:07,787 --> 00:06:09,716
- We wanted to make it the biggest. - Yes.

175
00:06:09,717 --> 00:06:11,326
But we still had to bring it in.

176
00:06:11,327 --> 00:06:13,897
- Remember how we measured the door? - Yes.

177
00:06:14,027 --> 00:06:16,497
This was the biggest size your door allowed.

178
00:06:16,657 --> 00:06:18,667
The size must be over 400.

179
00:06:19,097 --> 00:06:20,837
The height is 3m.

180
00:06:21,537 --> 00:06:22,737
The width is 2m.

181
00:06:23,607 --> 00:06:24,907
That's huge indeed.

182
00:06:25,707 --> 00:06:27,536
I love it because it looks like a painting.

183
00:06:27,537 --> 00:06:28,806
- Right. - Yes.

184
00:06:28,807 --> 00:06:31,947
I didn't use the regular materials for this. I got it on a canvas.

185
00:06:32,177 --> 00:06:33,177
(No wonder!)

186
00:06:33,178 --> 00:06:35,176
- It looks like a painting more so. - I see.

187
00:06:35,177 --> 00:06:37,216
- You've painted several times. - Right.

188
00:06:37,217 --> 00:06:39,647
It has a similar texture from that.

189
00:06:40,047 --> 00:06:41,157
This is great.

190
00:06:43,117 --> 00:06:44,826
(The last step of pulling it upright keeps them busy.)

191
00:06:44,827 --> 00:06:46,887
(Glancing)

192
00:06:47,827 --> 00:06:50,427
Do you need help?

193
00:06:50,967 --> 00:06:52,626
- When it's time to move this, - Yes.

194
00:06:52,627 --> 00:06:55,237
I'd like to have a guy helping me.

195
00:06:55,467 --> 00:06:57,266
I'm stronger than an average guy.

196
00:06:57,267 --> 00:06:58,867
Don't you think I can help you?

197
00:06:59,207 --> 00:07:00,607
- Sure. - Here comes Na Rae.

198
00:07:01,207 --> 00:07:03,707
- You're dressed for a battle. - Right. My gosh.

199
00:07:04,207 --> 00:07:05,346
I can totally help him.

200
00:07:05,347 --> 00:07:06,647
(Sergeant Park sets out to back him up.)

201
00:07:07,877 --> 00:07:08,877
(Opening)

202
00:07:09,377 --> 00:07:11,617
(She wears a helmet for safety.)

203
00:07:12,417 --> 00:07:14,257
- She's got everything at home. - She has everything.

204
00:07:14,517 --> 00:07:16,086
I didn't know she had a helmet at home.

205
00:07:16,087 --> 00:07:17,586
- The helmet... - I have one too.

206
00:07:17,587 --> 00:07:19,827
- I have 3 helmets. - Why do you have 3 helmets?

207
00:07:20,057 --> 00:07:21,696
- I ride the bicycle. - I see, the bicycle.

208
00:07:21,697 --> 00:07:24,126
Depending on my mood, I sometimes want to wear...

209
00:07:24,127 --> 00:07:26,696
a pink one, a white one, or a black one.

210
00:07:26,697 --> 00:07:28,737
- That's important. Yes. - Right. Yes.

211
00:07:29,967 --> 00:07:32,037
(She wears the military helmet to match with her personal color.)

212
00:07:32,407 --> 00:07:34,037
Why does the helmet look like a combat helmet?

213
00:07:35,137 --> 00:07:37,577
- "Trainee 156 Jun Hyun Moo." - Right.

214
00:07:38,007 --> 00:07:39,607
- You're wearing a helmet? - Of course.

215
00:07:40,207 --> 00:07:42,017
I have all kinds of tools at home.

216
00:07:42,547 --> 00:07:44,446
You have to hold that part. Could you help her?

217
00:07:44,447 --> 00:07:45,487
- Where? - Hold it.

218
00:07:46,047 --> 00:07:47,716
- Should I hold it here? - Do we have to lay it flat?

219
00:07:47,717 --> 00:07:49,417
- Yes. Come to the middle. - Okay.

220
00:07:50,787 --> 00:07:52,286
(Na Rae is supporting the back.)

221
00:07:52,287 --> 00:07:54,627
- I see. It's set up on the back. - Yes.

222
00:07:54,927 --> 00:07:56,527
Hold it like that. One, two.

223
00:07:56,897 --> 00:07:57,897
(Grunting)

224
00:07:57,898 --> 00:08:00,326
- Keep coming. - My gosh.

225
00:08:00,327 --> 00:08:01,367
Right here.

226
00:08:02,867 --> 00:08:05,437
(It's like moving a real art piece from a museum.)

227
00:08:06,107 --> 00:08:07,476
(Carefully moving it)

228
00:08:07,477 --> 00:08:08,477
Seriously.

229
00:08:08,977 --> 00:08:10,176
That's huge.

230
00:08:10,177 --> 00:08:11,547
(Four people are struggling to carry it due to the large size.)

231
00:08:12,977 --> 00:08:15,447
Turn it. Sir, you have to go that way.

232
00:08:16,077 --> 00:08:17,417
- They're turning it. - Yes.

233
00:08:17,987 --> 00:08:19,117
This is good.

234
00:08:20,617 --> 00:08:22,116
(Struggling)

235
00:08:22,117 --> 00:08:23,216
Hold on.

236
00:08:23,217 --> 00:08:24,226
(Amazed)

237
00:08:24,227 --> 00:08:25,286
(It looks even larger standing upright.)

238
00:08:25,287 --> 00:08:26,756
The giraffe looks small in comparison.

239
00:08:26,757 --> 00:08:27,727
Right.

240
00:08:27,927 --> 00:08:30,157
(Eye-catching)

241
00:08:30,357 --> 00:08:32,297
(Even Na Rae is amazed by the size.)

242
00:08:32,767 --> 00:08:34,997
(He places down the ladder after moving it.)

243
00:08:35,597 --> 00:08:36,597
(Rattling)

244
00:08:37,807 --> 00:08:40,037
(Pulling up)

245
00:08:40,507 --> 00:08:42,077
(Again?)

246
00:08:42,937 --> 00:08:44,506
(The ladder keeps on going up.)

247
00:08:44,507 --> 00:08:46,077
I used that ladder when I painted the mural.

248
00:08:46,907 --> 00:08:48,347
It's going up now. One, two.

249
00:08:50,377 --> 00:08:51,947
(The art piece is soaring higher.)

250
00:08:52,987 --> 00:08:55,416
I see. He has to drill in every hole for this.

251
00:08:55,417 --> 00:08:57,956
They will have to attach it firmly.

252
00:08:57,957 --> 00:08:58,987
They must secure it firmly.

253
00:08:59,327 --> 00:09:00,927
Who usually orders a canvas as big as this?

254
00:09:02,727 --> 00:09:04,196
- The NMK. - Pardon?

255
00:09:04,197 --> 00:09:05,766
The NMK.

256
00:09:05,767 --> 00:09:06,926
The NMK?

257
00:09:06,927 --> 00:09:08,167
The National Museum of Korea.

258
00:09:09,537 --> 00:09:11,937
You'd see canvases as big as this at the museum.

259
00:09:12,337 --> 00:09:14,807
I'd see canvases as big as this at the museum?

260
00:09:15,607 --> 00:09:17,076
You could see a piece as big as this there.

261
00:09:17,077 --> 00:09:19,047
Of course. Or the Museum of Modern and Contemporary Art.

262
00:09:19,747 --> 00:09:21,577
(Amazed)

263
00:09:24,787 --> 00:09:27,156
I haven't seen a painting as big as that at exhibits.

264
00:09:27,157 --> 00:09:29,217
Same here. That's huge.

265
00:09:29,557 --> 00:09:31,757
When I decided to get this done,

266
00:09:32,027 --> 00:09:33,827
I also decided to do it exactly how I wanted.

267
00:09:34,257 --> 00:09:35,996
So I went all out with this.

268
00:09:35,997 --> 00:09:37,296
Right. It's better to do it with your photo,

269
00:09:37,297 --> 00:09:38,997
- not someone else's. - But this is so cool. I mean it.

270
00:09:39,297 --> 00:09:40,397
Thanks.

271
00:09:41,297 --> 00:09:43,767
Now, I understand why they invited Lee Jung today.

272
00:09:43,907 --> 00:09:45,066
Without her...

273
00:09:45,067 --> 00:09:46,967
She's the only one who will side with Na Rae.

274
00:09:47,067 --> 00:09:48,436
But think about it.

275
00:09:48,437 --> 00:09:50,177
It's a healthy way of spending money.

276
00:09:51,477 --> 00:09:52,806
I could be small,

277
00:09:52,807 --> 00:09:54,146
- but I could dream big, right? - Yes.

278
00:09:54,147 --> 00:09:55,617
- Of course. - Yes.

279
00:09:56,247 --> 00:09:57,917
It looks so holy.

280
00:09:59,287 --> 00:10:01,287
Gosh, it's making me feel holy.

281
00:10:02,317 --> 00:10:04,457
It made me think at least I ought to love myself.

282
00:10:04,587 --> 00:10:05,587
(Chuckling)

283
00:10:06,487 --> 00:10:07,696
How about a lighting ceremony?

284
00:10:07,697 --> 00:10:08,757
A lighting ceremony.

285
00:10:08,897 --> 00:10:10,596
- Should I proceed? - Yes.

286
00:10:10,597 --> 00:10:11,897
Everyone, look forward to it.

287
00:10:12,067 --> 00:10:13,327
I really can't wait.

288
00:10:13,627 --> 00:10:15,996
(This looks like a real opening ceremony.)

289
00:10:15,997 --> 00:10:17,307
I'm curious.

290
00:10:17,907 --> 00:10:20,267
(A nerve-racking moment)

291
00:10:21,107 --> 00:10:23,506
(With everyone watching...)

292
00:10:23,507 --> 00:10:25,077
(Focused)

293
00:10:26,277 --> 00:10:28,917
(it's almost time for the highlight.)

294
00:10:30,817 --> 00:10:33,416
(At last!)

295
00:10:33,417 --> 00:10:34,717
(The lights are on.)

296
00:10:35,917 --> 00:10:37,287
(Impressed)

297
00:10:40,227 --> 00:10:42,697
(Giant Broom-to-be)

298
00:10:46,597 --> 00:10:47,897
- Gosh. - Oh, my.

299
00:10:48,097 --> 00:10:50,297
(Flashy lights)

300
00:10:50,667 --> 00:10:52,107
Na Rae became a deity.

301
00:10:53,737 --> 00:10:56,007
(Naphrodite has descended with a flash.)

302
00:10:59,407 --> 00:11:00,776
- Thank you so much. - Seeing it light up...

303
00:11:00,777 --> 00:11:02,176
must have made you feel good.

304
00:11:02,177 --> 00:11:03,987
Thank you.

305
00:11:04,387 --> 00:11:05,487
- Thank you. - Sure.

306
00:11:05,747 --> 00:11:08,187
Sir. I have a question.

307
00:11:08,387 --> 00:11:10,287
Do you have a title for this piece?

308
00:11:10,587 --> 00:11:12,056
You can make the sentence...

309
00:11:12,057 --> 00:11:13,557
- on the canvas the title. - What does it say?

310
00:11:13,727 --> 00:11:15,997
- "I'm grateful to be myself." - Yes.

311
00:11:17,127 --> 00:11:18,497
That's all about self-esteem.

312
00:11:18,697 --> 00:11:20,366
"I'm grateful to be myself."

313
00:11:20,367 --> 00:11:21,436
That's right.

314
00:11:21,437 --> 00:11:23,166
- "I'm happy that I'm me." - Yes.

315
00:11:23,167 --> 00:11:24,567
- That's right. - Right.

316
00:11:24,767 --> 00:11:26,867
It makes me so happy that I'm Park Na Rae.

317
00:11:27,407 --> 00:11:28,607
Yes.

318
00:11:28,877 --> 00:11:30,877
It makes me so happy that I'm Park Na Rae.

319
00:11:31,507 --> 00:11:32,877
Yes.

320
00:11:33,507 --> 00:11:34,777
Why are you laughing at me?

321
00:11:35,917 --> 00:11:37,647
Can I say something discouraging?

322
00:11:37,847 --> 00:11:38,916
What is it?

323
00:11:38,917 --> 00:11:40,987
People with real high self-esteem won't do things like that.

324
00:11:41,457 --> 00:11:42,587
You...

325
00:11:43,117 --> 00:11:44,187
He got me.

326
00:11:45,087 --> 00:11:46,926
But this is all great if this is a step...

327
00:11:46,927 --> 00:11:48,756
- toward high self-esteem. - Right.

328
00:11:48,757 --> 00:11:50,526
- Really? - It's gorgeous.

329
00:11:50,527 --> 00:11:51,697
The overwhelming sensation...

330
00:11:52,067 --> 00:11:53,997
- It's beautiful. - The colors look so pretty.

331
00:11:55,067 --> 00:11:56,437
Gosh, it came out so nicely.

332
00:11:57,637 --> 00:12:00,137
(It's still captivating even from afar.)

333
00:12:01,607 --> 00:12:02,677
This is amazing.

334
00:12:04,947 --> 00:12:07,347
(I'm hooked on myself.)

335
00:12:07,877 --> 00:12:11,347
I do think you're ahead of your time.

336
00:12:11,647 --> 00:12:13,146
It does have that cyberpunk feel.

337
00:12:13,147 --> 00:12:15,517
- Cyberpunk? - That's right.

338
00:12:15,687 --> 00:12:17,826
- Can your electricity support it? - Yes.

339
00:12:17,827 --> 00:12:19,386
- Can it handle the sign? - Yes, totally.

340
00:12:19,387 --> 00:12:21,296
This is a misconception about neon signs.

341
00:12:21,297 --> 00:12:22,956
People assume the signs eat up a lot of electricity.

342
00:12:22,957 --> 00:12:24,766
- Right. - It consumes as much electricity...

343
00:12:24,767 --> 00:12:26,567
- as half a fridge section. - Right.

344
00:12:27,127 --> 00:12:28,767
I didn't know it was energy-efficient.

345
00:12:28,837 --> 00:12:30,567
I don't know how other people will see it.

346
00:12:30,667 --> 00:12:32,707
But it made me feel solemn and gave off a holy vibe.

347
00:12:33,137 --> 00:12:35,977
It's the peak of self-esteem.

348
00:12:36,277 --> 00:12:39,906
Hyun Moo always displays his trophies at home.

349
00:12:39,907 --> 00:12:41,346
He blows up his own picture.

350
00:12:41,347 --> 00:12:42,447
Gosh, what's wrong with my face?

351
00:12:43,377 --> 00:12:44,687
Nothing compares to this.

352
00:12:45,247 --> 00:12:48,157
This... How is he going to copy this?

353
00:12:48,417 --> 00:12:50,286
This could be hanging at the NMK.

354
00:12:50,287 --> 00:12:51,687
(This is my own National Museum.)

355
00:12:52,557 --> 00:12:53,787
This could be hanging at the NMK.

356
00:12:54,197 --> 00:12:56,726
In other words, this is my treasure as well as Korea's.

357
00:12:56,727 --> 00:12:57,797
That's what it is to me.

358
00:12:58,197 --> 00:13:00,597
(The history of Korea is embedded at the National Museum of Korea.)

359
00:13:01,597 --> 00:13:03,836
(Just like the precious treasures in the museum...)

360
00:13:03,837 --> 00:13:04,967
They'll insert this to her clip?

361
00:13:05,467 --> 00:13:08,107
(A massive neon-sign piece of Na Rae at her house)

362
00:13:09,177 --> 00:13:11,406
(Treasure 1 of Na Rae's Museum)

363
00:13:11,407 --> 00:13:12,946
It could be hanging at a museum...

364
00:13:12,947 --> 00:13:14,477
- a few centuries later. - Right.

365
00:13:15,277 --> 00:13:18,217
When people come over to my house, I'll be their docent.

366
00:13:18,487 --> 00:13:20,487
"Let me explain to you about this piece."

367
00:13:20,757 --> 00:13:25,157
"It shows that I overcame all of the crises from the world."

368
00:13:25,787 --> 00:13:29,896
"The more you carve it, the more beautiful it becomes."

369
00:13:29,897 --> 00:13:31,597
I'll scam people that I drew that.

370
00:13:32,697 --> 00:13:34,437
(Do you understand?)

371
00:13:34,567 --> 00:13:36,937
Aphrodite was born from a shell.

372
00:13:37,137 --> 00:13:39,937
Na Rae is blooming from the shell.

373
00:13:40,467 --> 00:13:41,807
"Keep taking shots at me."

374
00:13:41,937 --> 00:13:43,107
"I'm Shelly Broom-to-be."

375
00:13:44,047 --> 00:13:45,106
What does that mean?

376
00:13:45,107 --> 00:13:46,777
"Na Rae who was born like a pearl."

377
00:13:47,247 --> 00:13:49,717
- I see. So you got an upgrade? - Yes.

378
00:13:50,277 --> 00:13:53,386
- Gosh, that sounds a bit off. - "Shelly Broom-to-be?"

379
00:13:53,387 --> 00:13:56,956
Here's an explanation. I'm the descendant of Hyeok Geo Se.

380
00:13:56,957 --> 00:13:58,026
- You? - The man who hatched from an egg.

381
00:13:58,027 --> 00:14:00,486
- Since you have the same last name? - Right. Of course.

382
00:14:00,487 --> 00:14:02,397
- So I'm Shelly Broom-to-be. - Shelly Broom-to-be?

383
00:14:02,627 --> 00:14:05,496
By the way, you do things on a different scale.

384
00:14:05,497 --> 00:14:06,726
- That's the worst-sounding name. - It looks beautiful too.

385
00:14:06,727 --> 00:14:08,196
- Thank you. I knew it. - Shelly Broom-to-be?

386
00:14:08,197 --> 00:14:09,266
- A lot of people want to do it. - Shelly Broom-to-be?

387
00:14:09,267 --> 00:14:10,296
- But they just can't. - I have...

388
00:14:10,297 --> 00:14:11,436
- a good eye for things like this. - Lee Jung.

389
00:14:11,437 --> 00:14:12,567
Are you cool with the name?

390
00:14:12,907 --> 00:14:13,683
Shelly Broom-to-be?

391
00:14:14,007 --> 00:14:17,007
- I'm okay as long as she's happy. - I'm so happy.

392
00:14:17,337 --> 00:14:19,376
I'm happy to be myself.

393
00:14:19,377 --> 00:14:20,407
(She barely manages to sympathize.)

394
00:14:21,017 --> 00:14:23,047
- Please wrap this up for me. - Sure.

395
00:14:23,517 --> 00:14:24,987
This is great because we can't see your face clearly.

396
00:14:25,847 --> 00:14:27,047
Come on.

397
00:14:27,117 --> 00:14:30,187
(After the installation, she comes out with arm sleeves.)

398
00:14:31,327 --> 00:14:33,356
(She's also carrying the cleaning equipment too.)

399
00:14:33,357 --> 00:14:35,427
(What's she going to do next?)

400
00:14:36,057 --> 00:14:37,566
Are you done with the installation?

401
00:14:37,567 --> 00:14:39,597
Yes. They're cleaning up.

402
00:14:40,167 --> 00:14:41,237
Yes.

403
00:14:42,237 --> 00:14:43,467
They sent out some military support.

404
00:14:45,337 --> 00:14:48,337
(Like the reliable soldiers from the military support...)

405
00:14:49,807 --> 00:14:51,847
My gosh. Seriously.

406
00:14:52,707 --> 00:14:54,547
This will be the peak season for mosquitoes.

407
00:14:54,677 --> 00:14:57,917
I have to spray the mosquito and larvae repellent.

408
00:14:58,217 --> 00:14:59,847
I need to water my crops.

409
00:15:00,017 --> 00:15:01,917
Persimmons keep on falling off.

410
00:15:02,357 --> 00:15:03,917
This is the busiest season for me.

411
00:15:04,557 --> 00:15:05,686
(Sighing)

412
00:15:05,687 --> 00:15:09,097
- This is when I have the most work. - This is the peak season.

413
00:15:10,397 --> 00:15:12,727
(She feels intimidated even before she begins.)

414
00:15:12,927 --> 00:15:14,466
What a mess, seriously.

415
00:15:14,467 --> 00:15:15,467
(What a mess, seriously.)

416
00:15:16,467 --> 00:15:18,637
(Na Rae's ultimate dream while having a garden)

417
00:15:19,237 --> 00:15:21,707
(The open-air bathtub with clear, pure water)

418
00:15:21,837 --> 00:15:23,577
Gosh, this is the perfect size.

419
00:15:24,277 --> 00:15:25,547
I was so happy then.

420
00:15:25,947 --> 00:15:27,006
(Right now, it's a mess.)

421
00:15:27,007 --> 00:15:28,147
Gosh. Oh, no.

422
00:15:28,347 --> 00:15:29,446
What's that?

423
00:15:29,447 --> 00:15:30,846
(Covered in leaves and dust)

424
00:15:30,847 --> 00:15:32,417
Why didn't you put a cover on it?

425
00:15:32,617 --> 00:15:33,747
It's much messier than I expected.

426
00:15:34,987 --> 00:15:36,887
(This looks worse than I anticipated.)

427
00:15:37,857 --> 00:15:39,687
What a royal mess.

428
00:15:41,157 --> 00:15:42,556
- What will you do with this? - Gosh.

429
00:15:42,557 --> 00:15:44,867
This is how it looks when I don't clean for ten days.

430
00:15:45,367 --> 00:15:47,166
- They're rotten. - They kept falling off.

431
00:15:47,167 --> 00:15:49,696
(The persimmons on the ground are rotting away.)

432
00:15:49,697 --> 00:15:50,937
What could you do?

433
00:15:52,067 --> 00:15:53,206
Gosh, this...

434
00:15:53,207 --> 00:15:54,277
(Brimming with resentment toward the persimmon tree)

435
00:15:54,737 --> 00:15:56,406
(She was happy when she was enjoying the fruit.)

436
00:15:56,407 --> 00:15:57,946
We plucked so many of those...

437
00:15:57,947 --> 00:15:59,446
- two years ago. - Right.

438
00:15:59,447 --> 00:16:01,077
Nothing is going right without Kian84.

439
00:16:01,777 --> 00:16:02,877
I'm sorry.

440
00:16:04,917 --> 00:16:06,847
(It's time to cherish the garden like she cherishes herself.)

441
00:16:07,557 --> 00:16:09,017
This is completely ruined.

442
00:16:10,257 --> 00:16:13,487
It's way too hot in summer, so it's impossible to use that.

443
00:16:13,687 --> 00:16:15,257
I don't go out there in summer.

444
00:16:16,527 --> 00:16:19,227
(It's fully covered with leaves and dust.)

445
00:16:20,067 --> 00:16:22,336
(Na Rae tries to sweep up the leaves.)

446
00:16:22,337 --> 00:16:23,767
When will you finish everything?

447
00:16:24,967 --> 00:16:26,666
How will you remove the moss?

448
00:16:26,667 --> 00:16:27,776
That?

449
00:16:27,777 --> 00:16:31,477
I can use a toothbrush.

450
00:16:32,477 --> 00:16:33,577
A toothbrush?

451
00:16:33,707 --> 00:16:37,417
A long time ago, people used to put toothpaste on a toothbrush...

452
00:16:37,717 --> 00:16:38,787
To clean?

453
00:16:39,117 --> 00:16:40,687
Have you served in the military?

454
00:16:40,887 --> 00:16:42,616
I used to do that when the commander was coming.

455
00:16:42,617 --> 00:16:43,787
How does she know all this?

456
00:16:44,227 --> 00:16:47,057
It started out as a joke. But how do you know all this?

457
00:16:47,457 --> 00:16:49,357
She remembers the military service better than us.

458
00:16:50,357 --> 00:16:52,167
- That's a detailed memory. - How do you know that?

459
00:16:53,197 --> 00:16:55,967
(Just like how she was taught in the military,)

460
00:16:56,337 --> 00:16:58,637
(she digs up the leaves from a hard-to-reach corner.)

461
00:16:59,467 --> 00:17:01,636
(She gets down on the ground and collects the leaves.)

462
00:17:01,637 --> 00:17:02,706
Darn it.

463
00:17:02,707 --> 00:17:04,037
(Groaning)

464
00:17:04,577 --> 00:17:06,477
(As she endures the late summer's heat...)

465
00:17:08,317 --> 00:17:10,877
Gosh. I mean, it looked great when she set it up.

466
00:17:13,117 --> 00:17:15,857
Darn it. This is so annoying.

467
00:17:16,887 --> 00:17:18,057
This is annoying.

468
00:17:18,857 --> 00:17:19,987
Hold on.

469
00:17:20,857 --> 00:17:22,126
This isn't going anywhere.

470
00:17:22,127 --> 00:17:23,997
(This isn't going anywhere.)

471
00:17:25,267 --> 00:17:28,667
(Hold on, let me think.)

472
00:17:29,537 --> 00:17:32,567
(I should try using this.)

473
00:17:33,407 --> 00:17:35,137
I'm finally using it after neglecting it for two years.

474
00:17:36,177 --> 00:17:37,177
(An excellent idea that could help her cleaning)

475
00:17:37,178 --> 00:17:38,346
Yes. I was wondering...

476
00:17:38,347 --> 00:17:39,476
- why you didn't use that. - What's that?

477
00:17:39,477 --> 00:17:40,876
- It's an air blower. - An air blower.

478
00:17:40,877 --> 00:17:42,116
(An air blower)

479
00:17:42,117 --> 00:17:43,817
I see. You have that at home.

480
00:17:44,077 --> 00:17:46,386
Here's the thing. To live in a house with a garden,

481
00:17:46,387 --> 00:17:47,486
- I... - You need that.

482
00:17:47,487 --> 00:17:49,556
- I can't hire people every time... - You really need that.

483
00:17:49,557 --> 00:17:51,326
- to get something done. - You must have that.

484
00:17:51,327 --> 00:17:52,587
You do things on a grand scale.

485
00:17:53,987 --> 00:17:55,557
(I can see hope.)

486
00:17:56,527 --> 00:17:57,727
Yes, just like that.

487
00:17:58,397 --> 00:18:00,297
Great. Gosh, this...

488
00:18:00,527 --> 00:18:02,597
Great. Gosh, this...

489
00:18:02,837 --> 00:18:03,937
What is this?

490
00:18:04,607 --> 00:18:05,966
- Do you have to charge it? - You must charge it.

491
00:18:05,967 --> 00:18:08,106
- No. Charging... - Did you not charge the battery?

492
00:18:08,107 --> 00:18:09,907
(What is it? What's wrong?)

493
00:18:10,707 --> 00:18:13,347
What? I had to buy the battery separately?

494
00:18:13,507 --> 00:18:15,576
Why didn't it come with the battery?

495
00:18:15,577 --> 00:18:17,946
You must have not added that while choosing the option.

496
00:18:17,947 --> 00:18:20,317
- Is that it? - It's always in the options.

497
00:18:20,687 --> 00:18:21,756
No wonder why it was so cheap.

498
00:18:21,757 --> 00:18:22,787
(It doesn't have a battery.)

499
00:18:22,887 --> 00:18:24,356
This would be perfect for this job.

500
00:18:24,357 --> 00:18:26,057
It's useless without the battery.

501
00:18:30,197 --> 00:18:33,497
Sir, do you have the same battery for this air blower?

502
00:18:33,797 --> 00:18:35,397
- Yes. - Thank you.

503
00:18:35,897 --> 00:18:37,296
- I'll return it quickly. - He had it.

504
00:18:37,297 --> 00:18:39,007
Gosh, you had it. Thank you.

505
00:18:39,537 --> 00:18:40,906
(Please work.)

506
00:18:40,907 --> 00:18:42,177
- Is it compatible? - Was it the perfect fit?

507
00:18:43,037 --> 00:18:44,106
Is it not working?

508
00:18:44,107 --> 00:18:45,947
- It's not compatible. - Really?

509
00:18:46,107 --> 00:18:47,276
Right, it can't be compatible.

510
00:18:47,277 --> 00:18:49,216
He had a professional one.

511
00:18:49,217 --> 00:18:50,847
But I didn't. So it didn't fit.

512
00:18:51,617 --> 00:18:53,946
Gosh, I have to do everything since I live here.

513
00:18:53,947 --> 00:18:55,017
(I'll have to sweep everything then.)

514
00:18:56,217 --> 00:18:59,157
(She tries her best to sweep up the leaves.)

515
00:19:00,357 --> 00:19:02,596
(There's no giving up despite the unfavorable condition.)

516
00:19:02,597 --> 00:19:03,597
This song...

517
00:19:05,497 --> 00:19:07,667
This is a familiar scene.

518
00:19:07,867 --> 00:19:09,666
That's why I feel awful when I see soldiers...

519
00:19:09,667 --> 00:19:10,967
shoveling snow.

520
00:19:12,667 --> 00:19:16,537
(She misses her fellow soldiers as she works alone.)

521
00:19:17,437 --> 00:19:19,906
Come on. There are cockroaches.

522
00:19:19,907 --> 00:19:20,977
Gosh, those house centipedes.

523
00:19:21,747 --> 00:19:23,376
- Cockroaches? - Yes.

524
00:19:23,377 --> 00:19:24,487
(I must overcome the cockroaches too.)

525
00:19:24,587 --> 00:19:25,746
Did you grab them with your hands?

526
00:19:25,747 --> 00:19:27,187
Of course, I had to use my hands.

527
00:19:27,647 --> 00:19:29,716
A piece of tissue and dead insects...

528
00:19:29,717 --> 00:19:31,286
- Of course. It's fine. - look about the same to us...

529
00:19:31,287 --> 00:19:32,427
because there are too many of them.

530
00:19:32,687 --> 00:19:34,056
I should clean proactively next time.

531
00:19:34,057 --> 00:19:35,927
(This time, she's cleaning the moss.)

532
00:19:36,827 --> 00:19:38,897
(Cleaning zealously)

533
00:19:39,697 --> 00:19:41,296
(The moss is coming off gradually.)

534
00:19:41,297 --> 00:19:42,937
Right, the tub was covered in moss.

535
00:19:43,067 --> 00:19:44,537
Gosh, it looks like matcha latte.

536
00:19:45,167 --> 00:19:46,207
Match latte?

537
00:19:46,407 --> 00:19:47,567
- That's a trendy drink now. - Yes.

538
00:19:50,037 --> 00:19:51,177
What do I do with this?

539
00:19:52,177 --> 00:19:53,207
It's clogged.

540
00:19:53,707 --> 00:19:55,216
- Of course, it will be clogged. - This is a disaster.

541
00:19:55,217 --> 00:19:56,447
(This is a disaster.)

542
00:19:56,977 --> 00:19:59,347
(What is this now?)

543
00:19:59,687 --> 00:20:01,787
(She didn't think the drain would be clogged.)

544
00:20:01,957 --> 00:20:03,456
- Why did it get clogged? - Gosh.

545
00:20:03,457 --> 00:20:04,556
- The leaves. - It was because of the leaves.

546
00:20:04,557 --> 00:20:05,986
Did they get tangled up in there?

547
00:20:05,987 --> 00:20:07,756
They didn't install a proper drain.

548
00:20:07,757 --> 00:20:09,427
I have to call in an expert.

549
00:20:09,927 --> 00:20:11,867
(She's sweating profusely.)

550
00:20:12,397 --> 00:20:14,126
I was going to use it starting next week.

551
00:20:14,127 --> 00:20:15,137
(The open-air bathtub is hopeless.)

552
00:20:16,597 --> 00:20:18,106
(She starts cleaning the fallen persimmons.)

553
00:20:18,107 --> 00:20:19,907
I should have invited Hyun Moo.

554
00:20:20,337 --> 00:20:21,406
If he saw this,

555
00:20:21,407 --> 00:20:22,637
he wouldn't have mentioned moving to a single-family home.

556
00:20:23,077 --> 00:20:24,507
I really wanted to call you over.

557
00:20:26,607 --> 00:20:30,077
(Hyun Moo has secretly been envious of Na Rae's garden.)

558
00:20:31,117 --> 00:20:33,517
(When he went to look at houses last time,)

559
00:20:34,617 --> 00:20:37,857
(he was fixated on single-family homes and gardens.)

560
00:20:37,887 --> 00:20:40,186
I don't mind having a lot of things to do. I can do them.

561
00:20:40,187 --> 00:20:41,526
But I hate bugs.

562
00:20:41,527 --> 00:20:42,657
This is the reality.

563
00:20:42,827 --> 00:20:44,297
(Conflicted)

564
00:20:45,327 --> 00:20:48,067
(The persimmons rotted while lying on the deck.)

565
00:20:48,637 --> 00:20:51,967
(She keeps raking them in as her face heats up.)

566
00:20:52,507 --> 00:20:54,006
They were stuck on the deck.

567
00:20:54,007 --> 00:20:55,507
- So it was hard to get them off. - Right, it's hard.

568
00:20:57,077 --> 00:21:00,847
(Nothing is easy about cleaning the garden.)

569
00:21:02,047 --> 00:21:03,417
(Retching)

570
00:21:04,147 --> 00:21:05,247
(Groaning)

571
00:21:06,117 --> 00:21:07,917
(Grossed out)

572
00:21:08,517 --> 00:21:11,527
(She can't stop gagging.)

573
00:21:12,427 --> 00:21:14,287
So listen.

574
00:21:15,027 --> 00:21:16,927
Gosh, thinking about it makes me nauseous again.

575
00:21:16,997 --> 00:21:18,097
- Oh, no. - The thought of it...

576
00:21:18,527 --> 00:21:20,426
The viewers will understand my reaction...

577
00:21:20,427 --> 00:21:22,237
once they can start smelling the same thing.

578
00:21:23,367 --> 00:21:25,367
Here's the thing about persimmons.

579
00:21:25,737 --> 00:21:28,677
They keep falling off from July to September.

580
00:21:28,907 --> 00:21:30,107
I'm talking about those tiny ones.

581
00:21:30,377 --> 00:21:32,246
If they just fall off, they wouldn't be my problem.

582
00:21:32,247 --> 00:21:33,846
- But they rot. - They get bruised.

583
00:21:33,847 --> 00:21:36,247
Then they get sour like persimmon vinegar.

584
00:21:36,547 --> 00:21:39,016
On top of that, they start growing fur...

585
00:21:39,017 --> 00:21:40,186
like fleece.

586
00:21:40,187 --> 00:21:42,086
- When they get moldy? - I see. They get moldy.

587
00:21:42,087 --> 00:21:44,527
Then they exude a rotten smell.

588
00:21:45,427 --> 00:21:46,727
(Gagging)

589
00:21:46,927 --> 00:21:48,226
(A blowfly follows her because of the smell.)

590
00:21:48,227 --> 00:21:50,027
My gosh, come on. That little jerk.

591
00:21:50,157 --> 00:21:51,967
- Seriously. - Blowflies keep coming to me.

592
00:21:52,367 --> 00:21:54,736
I thought the blowfly was a drone camera.

593
00:21:54,737 --> 00:21:56,537
- Those blowflies are huge. - It was as big as a drone camera?

594
00:21:56,597 --> 00:21:58,237
Seriously. It was this big.

595
00:21:58,337 --> 00:21:59,566
It must've been growing in her house.

596
00:21:59,567 --> 00:22:00,966
So they keep crashing into the window.

597
00:22:00,967 --> 00:22:02,636
Thud.

598
00:22:02,637 --> 00:22:04,006
- I'm serious. - That's true.

599
00:22:04,007 --> 00:22:05,146
- To come into your house? - I know what you're talking about.

600
00:22:05,147 --> 00:22:07,346
- Yes. - Those big ones...

601
00:22:07,347 --> 00:22:08,847
- They must be half-crazy. - Right.

602
00:22:09,517 --> 00:22:11,247
(But I hate blowflies.)

603
00:22:11,347 --> 00:22:12,486
- That's too much. - You're showing me this clip,

604
00:22:12,487 --> 00:22:13,587
so I don't move to a single-family home, right?

605
00:22:13,917 --> 00:22:14,987
No.

606
00:22:15,587 --> 00:22:17,117
(He still can't give up on a house with a garden.)

607
00:22:17,757 --> 00:22:18,986
I feel like throwing up.

608
00:22:18,987 --> 00:22:20,227
(The reality of living in a house with a garden)

609
00:22:21,327 --> 00:22:23,257
Should I pull out the tree for real?

610
00:22:25,197 --> 00:22:26,266
Should I just remove the tree?

611
00:22:26,267 --> 00:22:27,567
But you kept it on purpose.

612
00:22:27,727 --> 00:22:29,537
Right. It's 30 years old.

613
00:22:29,997 --> 00:22:31,267
Right, it's a magical tree.

614
00:22:33,367 --> 00:22:35,906
- Gosh, her house... - She has so many things at home.

615
00:22:35,907 --> 00:22:37,406
I really thought I might die that day.

616
00:22:37,407 --> 00:22:38,977
I see how you never have a day off.

617
00:22:40,607 --> 00:22:42,617
Let's try to think this brings me joy.

618
00:22:43,277 --> 00:22:44,717
I'm happy.

619
00:22:44,987 --> 00:22:46,547
Gosh, I'm very happy.

620
00:22:49,417 --> 00:22:50,617
What? Are they done?

621
00:22:51,017 --> 00:22:52,087
Sir.

622
00:22:52,457 --> 00:22:53,787
Did you finish?

623
00:22:54,087 --> 00:22:55,757
Gosh, thank you for everything.

624
00:22:56,197 --> 00:22:57,227
Okay.

625
00:22:57,657 --> 00:22:59,597
- Mr. Neon is leaving. - I'll get going.

626
00:23:00,067 --> 00:23:01,197
Thank you.

627
00:23:01,867 --> 00:23:04,437
Gosh, I'll take a short break.

628
00:23:06,867 --> 00:23:08,007
It's so cool inside.

629
00:23:09,307 --> 00:23:10,777
Gosh, my house is absolute paradise.

630
00:23:13,007 --> 00:23:14,576
(Na Rae grabs water and snack for her break.)

631
00:23:14,577 --> 00:23:16,546
I did that on an empty stomach.

632
00:23:16,547 --> 00:23:18,386
I started to retch toward the end.

633
00:23:18,387 --> 00:23:19,787
You'll get a dizzy spell.

634
00:23:21,817 --> 00:23:25,227
(She drags her fatigued body outside into the heat.)

635
00:23:30,227 --> 00:23:32,027
I should have the low-sugar jeolpyeon.

636
00:23:32,067 --> 00:23:33,866
- Oh, that? - So you know about that rice cake.

637
00:23:33,867 --> 00:23:35,167
Of course.

638
00:23:39,907 --> 00:23:42,607
(She quickly has a bite.)

639
00:23:44,277 --> 00:23:47,477
(She can't stop taking bites of it.)

640
00:23:48,047 --> 00:23:49,277
(Humming)

641
00:23:49,417 --> 00:23:50,977
(She grabs another one right away.)

642
00:23:52,247 --> 00:23:53,986
(Chewy texture)

643
00:23:53,987 --> 00:23:55,017
It's tasty.

644
00:23:57,857 --> 00:24:00,127
- It's peanut-butter flavored. - Peanut-butter flavored?

645
00:24:00,227 --> 00:24:02,096
- You should've brought one. - Gosh, I'm sorry.

646
00:24:02,097 --> 00:24:04,127
- You could've brought some. - Right.

647
00:24:04,967 --> 00:24:08,297
(I'll eat up all the tasty food by myself.)

648
00:24:09,437 --> 00:24:10,697
This is tasty.

649
00:24:10,937 --> 00:24:13,237
(Munching)

650
00:24:15,107 --> 00:24:16,907
She's eating it like it's a combat ration.

651
00:24:17,407 --> 00:24:18,947
A combat ration?

652
00:24:19,407 --> 00:24:20,477
It's tasty.

653
00:24:21,447 --> 00:24:22,846
They make good rice cakes.

654
00:24:22,847 --> 00:24:25,187
(The snack is right up Sergeant Park's alley.)

655
00:24:28,157 --> 00:24:30,717
(Let me go back to cleaning.)

656
00:24:36,157 --> 00:24:37,297
What's that?

657
00:24:41,067 --> 00:24:43,437
(Turning on the electric drill)

658
00:24:44,497 --> 00:24:47,167
(She keeps bringing in industrial tools.)

659
00:24:49,977 --> 00:24:51,237
Gosh, what's that?

660
00:24:54,177 --> 00:24:57,177
(She strides over after fully getting ready.)

661
00:24:57,917 --> 00:24:59,717
Gosh, this is a big mess.

662
00:25:00,328 --> 00:25:01,255
Darn.

663
00:25:01,757 --> 00:25:03,087
I paid a lot of money for this.

664
00:25:05,587 --> 00:25:06,657
What?

665
00:25:07,157 --> 00:25:09,227
(The pride of Korea, the National Museum of Korea)

666
00:25:10,357 --> 00:25:12,427
(National and Na Rae's Treasure 1, "Shelly Broom-to-be,")

667
00:25:13,527 --> 00:25:14,796
(is followed by Treasure 2.)

668
00:25:14,797 --> 00:25:16,296
As for the cauldron lid,

669
00:25:16,297 --> 00:25:19,236
I used it often during spring.

670
00:25:19,237 --> 00:25:21,167
I grilled pork belly on it.

671
00:25:21,537 --> 00:25:23,277
I also braised chicken in it.

672
00:25:23,877 --> 00:25:26,076
I should have brought it inside.

673
00:25:26,077 --> 00:25:28,247
But it got rusted during the rainy season.

674
00:25:28,547 --> 00:25:30,077
It really is a mess.

675
00:25:30,977 --> 00:25:32,347
Once it rusts,

676
00:25:32,617 --> 00:25:34,987
no matter how hard you try, you can't remove the rust.

677
00:25:35,587 --> 00:25:36,656
As for the tool I used,

678
00:25:36,657 --> 00:25:38,926
I could hook it up with the drill and use it on the lid.

679
00:25:38,927 --> 00:25:41,056
This can remove the rust.

680
00:25:41,057 --> 00:25:42,397
It becomes as good as new.

681
00:25:42,797 --> 00:25:44,696
- My gosh. - That? I see.

682
00:25:44,697 --> 00:25:47,196
I can grind the iron with that.

683
00:25:47,197 --> 00:25:49,337
- I see. You can remove a layer? - Yes.

684
00:25:49,537 --> 00:25:51,707
Gosh, it's coming off. This is amazing.

685
00:25:54,107 --> 00:25:55,877
(I'll knock you out.)

686
00:25:57,137 --> 00:25:58,507
This hits the spot.

687
00:26:00,607 --> 00:26:04,217
(She picks up the drilling speed.)

688
00:26:05,787 --> 00:26:06,817
The left side now.

689
00:26:07,547 --> 00:26:09,857
(It's time to rinse off the lid with a water hose.)

690
00:26:11,857 --> 00:26:12,926
It's not working.

691
00:26:12,927 --> 00:26:14,157
I need to do it four times.

692
00:26:18,867 --> 00:26:20,126
My gosh.

693
00:26:20,127 --> 00:26:21,296
It looks like a loach.

694
00:26:21,297 --> 00:26:23,236
That's like when dogs are happy to see people.

695
00:26:23,237 --> 00:26:24,767
(Turning)

696
00:26:25,237 --> 00:26:26,267
Darn.

697
00:26:27,507 --> 00:26:30,177
(She barely manages to turn off the water.)

698
00:26:31,507 --> 00:26:33,477
(She's at the end of her rope.)

699
00:26:34,877 --> 00:26:37,277
(But she must finish cleaning.)

700
00:26:39,317 --> 00:26:41,047
- More persimmons are falling off. - Gosh.

701
00:26:41,247 --> 00:26:42,457
Will you move into a single-family home?

702
00:26:43,457 --> 00:26:44,987
The idea started to sound less attractive.

703
00:26:48,457 --> 00:26:50,027
I can see that you're actually angry.

704
00:26:51,927 --> 00:26:52,927
My gosh.

705
00:26:52,928 --> 00:26:54,367
Come on. There it goes again.

706
00:26:54,467 --> 00:26:55,467
It's cute, though.

707
00:26:55,967 --> 00:26:57,136
You should raise it like a pet.

708
00:26:57,137 --> 00:26:58,467
How cute.

709
00:26:59,537 --> 00:27:00,736
(She barely manages to calm down her pet hose.)

710
00:27:00,737 --> 00:27:01,837
Let's just do it.

711
00:27:03,077 --> 00:27:07,907
(She starts rinsing off the deck with newfound determination.)

712
00:27:12,387 --> 00:27:14,487
(She tries to clean the cauldron lid again.)

713
00:27:16,087 --> 00:27:18,387
(Thoroughly washing it)

714
00:27:18,827 --> 00:27:21,426
Usually, it only takes two cycles to clean this completely.

715
00:27:21,427 --> 00:27:22,627
But it looks much better now.

716
00:27:24,867 --> 00:27:26,326
- It looks clean. - The rust...

717
00:27:26,327 --> 00:27:27,697
- is gone. Yes. - It looks good.

718
00:27:28,937 --> 00:27:31,267
(She finishes cleaning the yard after two hours.)

719
00:27:32,567 --> 00:27:35,307
(Drained)

720
00:27:36,007 --> 00:27:38,506
It wasn't just sunny that day, seriously.

721
00:27:38,507 --> 00:27:39,907
It was hot and humid. You know?

722
00:27:40,377 --> 00:27:42,177
On days like that, you sweat even when you do nothing.

723
00:27:43,677 --> 00:27:46,917
(I gave my all.)

724
00:27:47,687 --> 00:27:49,487
(Grabbing)

725
00:27:53,487 --> 00:27:54,587
What's that now?

726
00:27:54,627 --> 00:27:56,457
- A sprinkler to water the plants. - Is it a sprinkler?

727
00:27:57,897 --> 00:27:59,927
(Gushing out)

728
00:28:03,597 --> 00:28:05,637
What's she doing? Is she taking it off?

729
00:28:07,967 --> 00:28:09,436
(She approaches it with a determined look.)

730
00:28:09,437 --> 00:28:10,977
(Smirking)

731
00:28:11,437 --> 00:28:12,976
(She splashes it on herself, not the plants.)

732
00:28:12,977 --> 00:28:14,677
You wash off your body outdoors more than men.

733
00:28:16,617 --> 00:28:18,586
(She washes her hair boldly like a confident woman.)

734
00:28:18,587 --> 00:28:20,646
(Or is she washing her waist?)

735
00:28:20,647 --> 00:28:22,157
- Gosh. What's that? - My gosh.

736
00:28:22,517 --> 00:28:24,816
This must be your vacation seeing how you have a swim ring on.

737
00:28:24,817 --> 00:28:25,826
- You look like the Michelin Man. - Yes.

738
00:28:25,827 --> 00:28:26,986
- You went on your summer vacation. - Yes.

739
00:28:26,987 --> 00:28:29,926
My gosh. But everyone has some love handles.

740
00:28:29,927 --> 00:28:32,227
I like this side of myself too. I find myself lovable.

741
00:28:32,367 --> 00:28:34,297
No one said anything.

742
00:28:35,267 --> 00:28:37,667
She's desperately defending herself.

743
00:28:37,897 --> 00:28:40,207
- We didn't say anything. - Right. What?

744
00:28:41,907 --> 00:28:43,476
(Today,)

745
00:28:43,477 --> 00:28:44,977
(I'll spend the whole day, loving myself.)

746
00:28:46,177 --> 00:28:47,246
(Whizzing)

747
00:28:47,247 --> 00:28:48,977
(What's she doing?)

748
00:28:51,847 --> 00:28:53,086
(She's rinsing her teeth.)

749
00:28:53,087 --> 00:28:55,787
- Why is she rinsing her mouth here? - I can't believe I must watch this.

750
00:28:57,257 --> 00:29:00,057
(A refreshing and fun way to floss without lifting a finger)

751
00:29:00,187 --> 00:29:02,696
- It actually fell off. - You're right.

752
00:29:02,697 --> 00:29:03,827
What happened?

753
00:29:05,797 --> 00:29:07,827
(A remnant of the snack she just had)

754
00:29:10,497 --> 00:29:12,367
- A peanut particle flew off. - Yes.

755
00:29:12,967 --> 00:29:14,706
I see that it has many functions.

756
00:29:14,707 --> 00:29:16,576
- Of course. - It has a flossing function.

757
00:29:16,577 --> 00:29:18,307
(I see that it can remove food particles effectively.)

758
00:29:18,577 --> 00:29:20,106
Kian84, you should borrow it...

759
00:29:20,107 --> 00:29:21,547
to floss your buttocks.

760
00:29:21,917 --> 00:29:24,047
Okay. If you lend me that item, I'll use it on my buttocks.

761
00:29:24,577 --> 00:29:26,287
It'll be true friendship.

762
00:29:27,547 --> 00:29:29,657
(The cool flossing time ends.)

763
00:29:31,957 --> 00:29:34,187
- It was a solo Waterbomb Festival. - Yes.

764
00:29:35,557 --> 00:29:36,557
(My goodness)

765
00:29:39,897 --> 00:29:42,297
Gosh, what's up with these eggplants?

766
00:29:43,797 --> 00:29:45,867
How did I get so many eggplants?

767
00:29:46,567 --> 00:29:49,207
(The place Na Rae loves as much as herself)

768
00:29:50,277 --> 00:29:51,507
It's truly a mystery.

769
00:29:51,607 --> 00:29:55,447
There are only eight roots, but the eggplants come out sturdy.

770
00:29:56,217 --> 00:29:57,316
- They're sturdy. - What?

771
00:29:57,317 --> 00:29:58,447
How are they so big?

772
00:29:58,647 --> 00:29:59,986
Does gravity work differently...

773
00:29:59,987 --> 00:30:01,246
- in this house? - I don't know.

774
00:30:01,247 --> 00:30:02,517
They look like spoon worms.

775
00:30:03,687 --> 00:30:06,056
- Those eggplants look a bit scary. - They look so strange.

776
00:30:06,057 --> 00:30:08,127
- That's a boomerang. - It's so big.

777
00:30:08,287 --> 00:30:09,996
Who was the Japanese cartoon artist Kian84 liked?

778
00:30:09,997 --> 00:30:11,196
- He'd like those eggplants. - Ito Junji.

779
00:30:11,197 --> 00:30:12,396
Those eggplants have that vibe.

780
00:30:12,397 --> 00:30:13,466
At my place,

781
00:30:13,467 --> 00:30:14,596
- I just need to sow the seeds. - The soil must be nice.

782
00:30:14,597 --> 00:30:16,096
- The crops become huge. - They grow on their own.

783
00:30:16,097 --> 00:30:17,136
Seriously.

784
00:30:17,137 --> 00:30:20,267
The energy of the land must be nice because the plants grow well.

785
00:30:20,707 --> 00:30:23,607
